        comment     2026-11-12T10:32:15Z
            Rules for every brief (from core-task-briefs-2026-11-12.md, proposed)

            - **Spec first.** The seven runtime Specs in `wiki/specs/core/` (WriteLanes, Actors, WriteAhead, Restore,
              Reads, Turn, Codec) are the build's source. Where a Spec and the code disagree, the Spec wins; where a 
              Spec is
              silent, stop and ask. Shared terms: ending, Attempt, lane and owed row are WriteLanes'; Start and Life are
              Actors'; record kinds are WriteAhead R4 and R5.
            - **Every requirement is a failing check first.** Write the check and its wrong runtime, see the check red 
              on
              today's code (or, where today's code is already right, the wrong runtime red and the check green), commit
              that, then build. Say in the Task comment which of the two it was. Expected results come from the Spec 
              rule
              cited, never from running the code.
            - **Every Invariant the Task owns gets an oracle or case.** `conformance/not-yet.ts` lists the Invariants 
              each
              Task owns. The requirement lines are the ones with design content; every other owned Invariant still needs
              its oracle or case and a wrong runtime, written from its Spec text and its Spec test (T-numbered).
            - **Slice template** (T18, T19, T20; @epic.V3 instructions): the suite half (Spec change, model section, 
              parts,
              model mutation, audit) is green on the Spec model and reviewed before the production half starts; then
              production test-first; then the benchmark replay and the gates. "Declared, Enabled, Covered, Guards" in 
              each
              record's acceptance are that template's items and are kept verbatim.
            - **All fixtures are central** (the user, 2026-11-12). No check is "one app passes". Each requirement runs 
              over
              the generated parts available at its slice, under their usual and stress profiles, both world seeds:
              - "the base parts": the pilots S16 transfers, S21 sign-in and S10 sync 
                (`packages/flow-state-v3/fixtures/`),
                their app mixes (`MIX-*`), and the generated parts T52 makes of the hand fixtures (including Pay and
                checkout) and Cells;
              - built by T18, from
                code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/fixture-catalogue-2026-11-11.md: S01, S24 (composite A
                Editor); S08, S03, S22 flat (B Outbox); S12, S23 (C Control); S09 flat (G Optimistic);
              - built by T19: S13 (E Chat). S25 is @task.V3.T56's.

              A requirement line names the parts that reach it most directly; it still runs over all parts available. 
              The
              fixtures (including Pay and checkout) keep one more role: the type-error count against `src/index.ts`, 
              which
              checks the API, not behaviour.
            - **Store contents before the disk reader exists.** Until T19 builds the Port's disk reader, a check that 
              reads
              "store contents" reads the fake world's store from the harness side (`conformance/world/storage.ts`), 
              never
              through the runtime.
            - **Gates.** `nub run conformance:gates` runs them all and writes `conformance/gates.json`. Singly:
              `bun packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/runner/main.ts <mode>` with modes `suite`, `falsify`, `liar`, 
              `crash`
              (each `--runtime production`), `parts`, `pairs`, `matrix-gate`, `model-mutation`, `mutate` (core mutants),
              `coverage`, `regressions`. Then `nub run check:fix` and `nub run check` at zero errors and zero warnings.
              "No worse" means every gate's count at the Task's start, recorded in the Task's first comment.
            - **Benchmarks.** `packages/flow-state-v3/bench/bench.ts` replays a part and returns its work fingerprint 
              and
              turn costs; no runner mode calls it yet. A Task that needs a cost number adds the scenario there and runs 
              it
              alone (no other process), recording the command in its comment.
            - **Type tests** are `*.type-test.ts` files beside the code, using `@ts-expect-error`, checked by
              `nub run check:effect`; each is planted wrong once to see it fail for the expected reason.
            - **Shell.** Every command is bounded: `perl -e 'alarm N; exec @ARGV' -- <cmd> </dev/null`, under 3 
              minutes, no
              stdin reads; files are written with Write and Edit; kill your own stale shells. Suite slices and mutants 
              may
              run up to 8 in parallel; browsers and benchmarks run alone.
            - **Code style.** Read `tools/lint-bomb/STYLE.md`, `tools/lint-bomb/examples/style.ts` and
              `tools/lint-bomb/FLOW-STATE.md` before writing. The core (outside `src/host/`) is plain, pure, synchronous
              TypeScript returning `{ state, effects }`; the host uses Effect fully. No implicit state machine: a set of
              flags that together mean a state becomes one tagged value and one total table.
            - **Sidecars.** Haiku only, effort high, read-only unless given one disjoint file with an acceptance check; 
              the
              lead reads every line and re-runs every check. A reviewer sidecar checks each oracle, harness or runtime
              change.
            - **Traps that apply to every Task:**
              1. **A suite that cannot load once counted as killing mutants.** Fixed in `974ad3a1`: a kill counts only 
                 when
                 a failure names an oracle or a cited Invariant. Keep it guarded: for each new wrong runtime, read the
                 failure and confirm it names the oracle or Invariant its requirement lists. A load failure, defect,
                 timeout or missing verdict is a broken mutant, not a kill.
              2. **Which side is wrong.** A red on production may be the model's or the oracle's fault (T47 found four 
                 such:
                 GEN-pay-search-20, 21, 55 and seeded-173). Before changing production to turn a check green, derive the
                 expected result from the Spec rule by hand. If the model is wrong, fix the model and say so in the 
                 comment.
              3. **A lenient oracle passes everything.** Each oracle change needs its wrong runtime red before and 
                 after.
              4. **A differential baseline that silently re-records.** A baseline is re-recorded only with a named entry
                 that cites the pending case it flips.
            - **Never decide alone, in any Task:** a guarantee change (any Spec `must` or Invariant), a public name or 
              shape
              in `src/index.ts`, a new report kind, a new record kind or field, dropping or weakening a check, taking a
              documented fallback. Ask the orchestrator. Small names inside the core are the builder's.
            - **Close-out.** Each Task ends with a comment listing: gates at start and end, each allowed baseline 
              difference
              with its pending case, each Spec line changed (also in `wiki/specs/spec-log.md`), and the handoff items
              written as comments on the receiving Task.
            - **Names.** A name after "Wrong:" is the name to give a new wrong runtime or mutant; none exists yet. An 
              oracle
              or case marked (new) is written by the Task; one marked (exists) is in 
              `packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/`
              today. File names for new code inside `src/core/` and `src/host/` are the builder's unless given. Bare 
              rule
              ids carry their Spec's name on every line (WriteLanes I31, Actors T21); never drop it. If two lines 
              disagree,
              or a fact is missing, stop and ask the orchestrator; do not choose.
